---
term: Parent corporation
aka:
  - Parent
section: structure
order: 30
definition: A corporation whose members include other corporations, formed to pursue their [[higher-order common interest]].
seeAlso:
  - arbitration
  - power-balancing
  - constituent-corporation
  - conversion
---
A parent forms only through [[conversion]], when several parties convert a [[contract]] among them into a charter with its own organization. Each party ratifies the conversion under its own amendment procedure.

A parent corporation does the following:

- Aggregates constituent interests
- Coordinates constituent corporations
- Arbitrates conflicts
- Manages externalities
- Pursues the higher-order common interest

A parent can hold strong authority within its domain without absorbing every lower-level function. Its constituents continue to exist and aren't replaced by a central bureaucracy.
